    "result_text": "There's only one test class in the project (`AcceptanceTest`), and it passes along with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD membership tier rule: an owner becomes `GOLD` when its household (owners sharing the same `householdId`) has 3 or more members after the create; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules still apply.\n\nFollowing the codebase's existing `namesakeCount` idiom (a persisted count set by a pipeline step and read by the mapper), the changes are:\n\n1. **`CountHouseholdMembers.java`** (new function class) \u2014 runs after `AssignOwnerHousehold` (which sets/back-fills `householdId`) and before save. Sets `householdSize` = existing owners sharing the same `householdId` + 1 (the new, not-yet-persisted owner). A null `householdId` means a household of one.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `countHouseholdMembers` step into the pipeline between `countNamesakes` and `save`, under the same `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `householdSize` field (`@Column(name = \"household_size\")`) with getter/setter, so the value survives to GET time.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `membershipTier` expression now yields `GOLD` when `householdSize >= 3`, falling through to the unchanged SILVER/BRONZE logic otherwise (GOLD takes precedence, as the spec states).\n\n5. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `household_size` column. Tests run on hsqldb; existing data inserts use explicit column lists, so the addition is safe.\n\nBecause `householdSize` is persisted and read by the shared mapper, the tier is correct both in the create response and on subsequent `GET /api/owners/{id}` \u2014 which is exactly what the `coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` acceptance test asserts.",
